The generated StringHelper case tables use UnicodeData.txt and SpecialCasing.txt
13.0.0, 14.0.0, 15.0.0, 15.1.0 and 16.0.0 from the Unicode Consortium. Exact
original URLs, sizes and SHA-256 digests are in manifest.json. LICENSE-UNICODE
retains Unicode License v3. Runtime implementation and regeneration are Rust.

These are the already attributed inputs retained by PR77 at
074ef8782244b18240d199c887595247b9ac131d, not PR33. The JDK Character API records
bind JDK17–18 to Unicode13, JDK19 to14, JDK20–21 to15, JDK22–23 to15.1 and
JDK24–25 to16. See the official versioned Character documentation:
https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/17/docs/api/java.base/java/lang/Character.html
and corresponding release18 through25 paths.

The generated tables contain simple upper/lower mappings. For the leading
supplementary uppercase branch, these exact SpecialCasing inputs contain no
supplementary unconditional override. For first-unit US lowercasing the only
unconditional expansion is U+0130 to U+0069 U+0307, implemented explicitly.
The Rust generator validates both facts before publishing tables.

No default case-data version is inferred from the host Rust compiler, user
locale or Gradle runtime. Binding the pinned Android Studio Rabbit1 bundled
JBR remains pending. Original test fixtures are asserted for every supported
version; this does not establish that missing baseline binding or JVM runtime
comparison. Source data is not a non-Rust runtime component.
